# C/C++ Project Rules for Hello World Project

## Code Style
- Follow Google C/C++ Style Guide
- Use snake_case for variables and functions
- Use UPPER_CASE for constants and macros
- Always include header guards in .h files
- Document all functions with Doxygen-style comments
- Use 2 spaces for indentation (not tabs)
- Maximum line length: 80 characters

## Project Structure
- Source files go in src/
- Header files go in include/
- Build artifacts go in build/
- Use Makefile for building
- Keep build directory in .gitignore

## Best Practices
- Always check return values
- Use const where appropriate
- Initialize variables before use
- Handle errors gracefully
- Use meaningful variable names
- Include forward declarations for static functions
- Use static for functions that are only used within the same file

## Documentation Standards
- Use /** */ for function documentation
- Include @brief, @param, @return tags
- Document complex algorithms and data structures
- Add inline comments for non-obvious code
- Include file headers with @file, @brief, @author, @date

## Error Handling
- Use error_code_t enum for consistent error reporting
- Always check malloc/calloc return values
- Use DEBUG_PRINT macro for debug output
- Return appropriate error codes from functions
- Print error messages to stderr, not stdout

## Memory Management
- Always free allocated memory
- Use UNUSED_PARAM macro for unused parameters
- Avoid memory leaks
- Use appropriate data types for sizes (size_t, not int)

## Compiler Warnings
- Enable all warnings (-Wall -Wextra)
- Use -pedantic for strict C99 compliance
- Fix all warnings before committing
- Use -Werror in CI/CD if possible

## Testing
- Test all code paths
- Include edge cases
- Verify error handling
- Test with different input values
- Use debug mode for development

## Security
- Validate all user input
- Use bounds checking for arrays
- Avoid buffer overflows
- Use safe string functions when available
- Be careful with format strings in printf
